{"results":[{"url":"https://www.healthdata.org/research-analysis/gbd","final_url":"https://www.healthdata.org/research-analysis/gbd","title":"Global Burden of Disease (GBD)","description":"The GBD study quantifies health loss from hundreds of diseases, injuries, and risk factors, so that health systems can be improved.","language":"en","text":"# Global Burden of Disease (GBD)\n\nThe GBD study is the largest and most comprehensive effort to quantify health loss across places and over time, so health systems can be improved and disparities eliminated.\n\n607 billion+\nhighly standardized and comprehensive estimates measure health outcomes and systems.\n\n463\nhealth outcomes and risk factors provide a powerful basis for insights on global health trends and challenges.\n\n204\ncountries and territories, plus dozens of subnational locations show trends at regional, national, and local levels.\n\n20,000+\nindividuals from 167 countries and territories collaborate in vetting GBD data sources and estimates.\n\n###### On this page:\n\n## GBD Health State Survey – we need your help!\n\nIt only takes about 15 minutes.\n\nThe GBD study estimates the health impact of hundreds of diseases and injuries in every country in the world. A key input is how people judge the severity of different health conditions, from mild hearing loss to severe depression to the aftermath of a stroke. These judgments come directly from surveys like this one, so your responses feed into the estimates used by governments, WHO, and researchers worldwide.\n\nYou’ll be shown short descriptions of two health conditions at a time and asked which person you consider healthier. No medical background is needed, and it is available in 12 languages. Responses are anonymous.\n\nTake the survey\n\nPlease share the survey with anyone with an interest in global health. The more people who respond, from as many countries and backgrounds as possible, the stronger the estimates will be.\n\nThank you for your time and support.\n\n## Join the GBD Collaborator Network\n\nThe 2026 fall/winter application window is currently open, and we are accepting applications through **11:59 p.m., Pacific time on December 31, 2026**. Decisions for all applications submitted during this window will be sent to all applicants by March 31, 2027.\n\nApply today!\n\n## Featured\n\nThe latest global burden of diseases, injuries, and risk factors study (GBD) – an update through the year 2023 – provides a comprehensive picture of the world’s health following the COVID-19 pandemic.\n\n## More GBD publications\n\nView all\n\nScientific Publication\n\nMohsen Naghavi, Maegan Dirac, Nicole Weaver, Hannah Han, Aleksandr Aravkin, Xiaochen Dai, Erin Hamilton, Simon Hay, Tomislav Mestrovic, Ali Mokdad , Christopher J.L. Murray\n\nNovember 4, 2026\n\nScientific Publication\n\nNatalie Pritchett, Lisa Force, Marie Ng, Theo Vos, Miranda May, Jonathan Kocarnik, Kayleigh Bhangdia, Louise Penberthy, Xiaochen Dai, Simon Hay, Ali Mokdad , Sam Ostroff, Maja Pašović\n\nNovember 2, 2026\n\nScientific Publication\n\nLisa Force, Catherine Bisignano, Miranda May, Natalie Pritchett, Jonathan Kocarnik, Kayleigh Bhangdia, Louise Penberthy, Alistair Acheson, Xiaochen Dai, Sama Ghoba, Simon Hay, Tomislav Mestrovic, Ali Mokdad , Christopher J.L. Murray\n\nOctober 29, 2026\n\nScientific Publication\n\nDamian Santomauro, Aleksandr Aravkin, Catherine Bisignano, Xiaochen Dai, Sama Ghoba, Simon Hay, Tomislav Mestrovic, Paul Miller, Ali Mokdad , Christopher J.L. Murray\n\nOctober 28, 2026\n\n## GBD data and estimates\n\n## Explore GBD research\n\n### GBD 2023 Study Resources\n\nEvents, data tools, videos, and more – your guide to GBD 2023 starts here.\n\nLearn more about GBD 2023\n\n### Estimates by country\n\nSearch GBD results by national and subnational locations, and get an overview of population health factors like life expectancy, fertility, and causes of death.\n\nView country profiles\n\n### GBD for policy impact\n\nLearn how leaders in global health are using and contributing to the GBD study to make an impact on their communities with the GBD Emerging Researcher Award and Collaborator Spotlights.\n\nLearn about the award\n\nRead collaborator stories\n\n## About the GBD study\n\nEveryone, all over the world, deserves to live a long life in full health. One of the largest scientific collaborations in the world, the GBD measures what prevents us from achieving that goal, putting knowledge and tools into the hands of people and groups around the world to make people healthier.\n\nBy identifying the biggest health problems, it is helping governments, scientists, and partners advocate for resources and answer questions such as:\n\n* What diseases, injuries, and risk factors cause the most early death and disability in a given country?\n* How does health performance differ across countries?\n* When designing an intervention to improve the health of young women, which health problems should be targeted to make the greatest impact?\n\nLearn about the GBD study\n\nVisit the GBD Learning Center\n\n### Get involved\n\nCollaboration across the scientific community is one of our core principles. From data analysis to policy use, the GBD Collaborator Network is instrumental to the GBD study and affiliated research projects.\n\nJOIN THE NETWORK\n\n## News and events\n\nView all\n\nMedia mention\n\nMilenio\n\nOctober 31, 2025\n\nAnalysis\n\n#### County-specific disease burden in Norway\n\nFolkehelseinstituttet\n\nMay 31, 2024\n\nMedia mention\n\nNature\n\nMarch 31, 2026\n\nMedia mention\n\nCIDRAP\n\nOctober 27, 2026","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":330.1867359987227,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}